From: Tribhoon <amoapo0@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: defer REO TID cleanup until after NSS peer delete

With NSS Wi-Fi offload enabled, the NSS wifili firmware owns the
QCN6122 REO ring. ath11k_mac_station_remove() previously called
ath11k_dp_peer_cleanup() before ath11k_peer_delete().
ath11k_dp_peer_cleanup() issues HAL_REO_CMD_UPDATE_RX_QUEUE (VLD=0)
for every TID, marking the REO queue descriptors invalid in hardware.

When a client drops uncleanly (out-of-range, low ACKs, inactivity),
if those commands reach hardware while the NSS firmware still has
in-flight REO entries for the departing peer, the firmware's REO
processing loop stalls: it cannot advance past a descriptor whose
queue is now marked invalid. The stall is total: reo_reaped stops
advancing, rx_desc_in_use accumulates orphaned RXDMA buffers, and
the 5 GHz radio is dead until ath11k_ahb is reloaded.

Moving ath11k_dp_peer_cleanup() after ath11k_peer_delete() in
ath11k_mac_station_remove() fails because ath11k_peer_delete() ends
by waiting for the HTT PEER_UNMAP event, which deletes and frees the
peer struct (kfree(peer)). Calling dp_peer_cleanup() after
peer_delete() would find NULL from ath11k_peer_find() and silently
abort without invalidating or freeing the REO TID queues.

Instead, when NSS offload is enabled:
1. In ath11k_mac_station_remove(), skip the pre-delete dp_peer_cleanup
   (keeping the non-NSS path untouched).
2. In __ath11k_peer_delete(), call ath11k_dp_peer_cleanup() immediately
   after ath11k_nss_peer_delete() returns.

ath11k_nss_peer_delete() sends NSS_WIFILI_PEER_DELETE_MSG and waits
synchronously for the firmware's acknowledgment. By the time it
returns, the NSS firmware has completed peer deletion and drained all
in-flight work. The peer struct remains valid and active in the peer
list at this point, allowing ath11k_dp_peer_cleanup() to safely mark
the REO queues invalid in hardware and reclaim them before WMI peer
delete and HTT unmap free the peer.

Fixes: #53
Signed-off-by: Tribhoon <amoapo0@gmail.com>

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/mac.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/mac.c
@@ -10556,7 +10556,13 @@ static int ath11k_mac_station_remove(st
 		}
 	}
 
-	ath11k_dp_peer_cleanup(ar, arvif->vdev_id, sta->addr);
+	/*
+	 * For NSS Wi-Fi offload, dp_peer_cleanup is deferred until inside
+	 * __ath11k_peer_delete() after ath11k_nss_peer_delete() completes,
+	 * preventing in-flight REO descriptor stalls in NSS firmware.
+	 */
+	if (!ar->ab->nss.enabled)
+		ath11k_dp_peer_cleanup(ar, arvif->vdev_id, sta->addr);
 
 	ret = ath11k_peer_delete(ar, arvif->vdev_id, sta->addr);
 	if (ret)
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/peer.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/peer.c
@@ -737,6 +737,17 @@ static int __ath11k_peer_delete(struct a
 	reinit_completion(&ar->peer_delete_done);
 	ath11k_nss_peer_delete(ar, vdev_id, addr);
 
+	/*
+	 * With NSS Wi-Fi offload, the NSS wifili firmware owns the QCN6122 REO
+	 * ring. ath11k_nss_peer_delete() sends NSS_WIFILI_PEER_DELETE_MSG and
+	 * synchronously waits for the firmware's completion (handled by
+	 * ath11k_nss_peer_mem_free() once in-flight work has drained).
+	 * Invalidate REO TID queues now, while NSS firmware has finished with
+	 * this peer and before WMI delete / HTT unmap frees the peer struct.
+	 */
+	if (ar->ab->nss.enabled)
+		ath11k_dp_peer_cleanup(ar, vdev_id, addr);
+
 #ifdef CPTCFG_ATH11K_NSS_SUPPORT
 	mutex_lock(&ab->base_ast_lock);
 #endif
